  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 340 AM CDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nBy Friday, a longwave trough will be traversing the Intermountain \nWest working to initiate leeside cyclogenesis that will enhance \nsoutherly flow to the west of the region. At the same time, a \nsurface high will be sliding southward into the Upper Great Lakes \ncreating northeasterly flow into the boundary across the Ohio river \nvalley. The evolution of these features should favor a clockwise \npivot of this boundary with equatorward movement possible across \nportions of IL and points to the east. As a result, the boundary may \ncreep equatorward, back into the area, bringing chances of showers \nand thunderstorms with it. This may also keep the heat from \nexpanding to far to the north, potentially saving northeast MO/west-\ncentral IL from highs in the 90s Friday afternoon. Into the weekend, \nthe anomalous mid-level ridge centered over the region weakens as \nthe slow moving longwave trough out west approaches. Slow moving is \nthe key word here as an mid-level cut off low may halt the \nprogression of the feature. Guidance has continued to slow the \nevolution of the trough, which has led to increased confidence that \nheat continues Saturday and potentially into Sunday. The only bright \nside is that humidity levels appear to gradually lower into the \nweekend, which may lead to heat index values that are the same as \nthe ambient air temperature. Although, with potential for ambient \ntemperatures in the mid/upper 90s for some on Saturday, the lower \nmoisture won't provide much relief, if any.\n\nThe longwave trough out west is progged by long-range guidance to \neject into the Northern Plains Sunday night into Monday. The LREF \nIQR temperature spreads on Sunday and Monday are now both 10-15 \ndegrees, indicating this is when guidance expects a cold front to \nmove through the region. A slower front would lead to another \nunseasonably hot day for most while a faster front would result in a \nsooner arrival of real heat relief. Temperature IQR spreads decrease \nto 5-10 degrees Tuesday and beyond next week, but this time, \ncentered on values that would be near or slightly above \nclimatological normals. Values like this reveal the signal is there \nfor an end to the heat by the start of next week.",
